course: dictionary
lesson: 4723
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. Look at the words around the word first. «Hopeless» can mean without any hope, very bad at something, or impossible to solve. The sentence shows which one.
explain: «I'm hopeless at drawing» is not a sad sentence. The clue is «at drawing»: a skill comes after the word, so it means very bad at something. «A hopeless situation» is different, because there is no way out and no chance that things will get better.
explain: Swap in your meaning and check. «I'm very bad at drawing» fits, but «I'm without any hope at drawing» sounds wrong. If you ever feel hopeless for a long time, tell a trusted adult. Things can get better, and you do not have to carry it alone.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Hopeless» means without any hope, when there is no chance that things will get better.
fact: «Hopeless» can also mean very bad at something, as in «hopeless at passing».
fact: «Hopeless» can mean impossible to deal with or solve, as in «a hopeless mess».
fact: The suffix «-less» means without, so «hopeless» is the opposite of «hopeful», which means full of hope.
exercise: «Two years later Chloe wanted to get into the school football team and was hopeless at passing.» What does hopeless mean here? | Quick and clever | Stuck in a situation with no way out | *Very bad at something | Full of hope for the future
exercise: «Croquet with live animals is hopeless.» Which meaning fits best? | Very fast and exciting | Without any hope at all | *Impossible to deal with or solve | Quiet and calm
exercise: Which word is the opposite of «hopeless»? | useful | helpless | careless | *hopeful
test: Read «a hopeless situation». What does hopeless mean? | Very bad at cleaning up | Full of hope | Made of paper | *Without any hope, with no chance that things will get better
test: How is the suffix «-ful» spelled in «hopeful»? | with two l's | with no l | *with one l | with a y
exam: Lily says, «I feel hopeless.» She has felt this way for a long time. What should she do? | Wait for the word to change its meaning | Keep it secret and never speak about it | Decide that hopeless means happy | *Talk to a trusted adult, because things can get better
